# Fail Loudly, Never Plausibly

two defect shapes cost far more to find than to prevent, because both produce output that looks correct.

## a work queue must never drop an entry in silence

`ChunkSavingSystem` popped a coordinate off the save queue, found the chunk was not `Ready`, and discarded it — no retry, no log. purely generated chunks therefore never persisted at all, indefinitely, and nothing anywhere said so. the ordering bug that stranded them was one defect; the silence was a second, independent one, and it is the reason the first survived so long.

when you pop work you cannot complete right now, pick one of these out loud:

- **don't enqueue it** — if it was never meant to be written, keep it out of the queue in the first place;
- **put it back**, with a bounded attempt count;
- **drop it and `error!`**, naming the entry and what was lost.

there is no fourth option. an entry that can vanish without a trace is a defect on its own terms, whatever ordering bug put it there. the chunk pipeline's queues (generation, meshing, lighting, updates, saves) are where this matters most.

## an api must not return a plausible-but-meaningless value

a value that is well-formed, in range, and computed from nothing (a default standing in for "unknown", a ratio over a zero volume) is a lie a test can sit red on for a long time while it looks like a stale threshold.

make the wrong thing unrepresentable instead of defaulting past it: prefer `Option`/`Result` to a sentinel, and two types to one type with a field that is sometimes garbage, so a caller that lacks the input cannot be handed an answer.
